---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { approaches, prerequisiteNotice, prerequisites, prerequisiteTitle, products, relatedGuidance } from '../data/guidance';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
---
<Layout title="Compare agent platforms (Draft)">
  <div class="page-shell comparison-page">
    <a class="back-link" href={withBase()}>Back to the guide</a>
    <section class="intro">
      <h1>Compare agent platforms (Draft)</h1>
      <p>Review the platforms currently covered in each approach, including their intended scenarios, implementation responsibilities, limitations, and prerequisites.</p>
    </section>
    <div class="guidance-notice"><span class="draft-badge">Draft guidance</span><p>These descriptions reflect the supplied decision table. Confirm current platform capabilities before development.</p></div>
    {approaches.map(approach => (
      <section class="comparison-approach" aria-labelledby={`approach-${approach.id}`}>
        <h2 id={`approach-${approach.id}`}>{approach.label}</h2>
        <p class="muted">{approach.scope}</p>
        <div class="comparison-list">
      {products.filter(product => approach.products.includes(product.id)).map(product => (
        <article class="comparison-item" id={product.id}>
          <div class="product-heading"><span class="utility">{product.category}</span><h3>{product.name}</h3><p>{product.summary}</p></div>
          <div><h4>Intended scenarios</h4><p>{product.goodFor}</p><h4>Limitations and responsibilities</h4><ul>{product.watch.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul><div class="review-note"><h4>Items to validate</h4><ul>{product.review.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul></div><h4>{prerequisiteTitle}</h4><p>{prerequisiteNotice}</p><ul>{prerequisites[product.id].map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul></div>
        </article>
      ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
    <section id="about" class="methodology">
      <span class="utility">About this guidance</span>
      <h2>How recommendations are determined</h2>
      <p>The guide begins with three approaches, each covering two platforms today. Category membership defines the scope of this guidance, rather than a universal capability limitation. Organization-designed automation may include deeper-customization options in future; that combination is not currently covered here.</p>
      <h3>Route-specific decision criteria</h3>
      <p>Employee-guided work asks which jobs are required. Every selected job adds requirements: knowledge and guidance can fit Agent Builder, while business-system actions or complex tasks narrow to Cowork. When Agent Builder remains, a question about unprompted work distinguishes it from Cowork. Required Cowork plugins and scheduling remain subject to confirmation.</p>
      <p>Organization-designed business process automation covers repeatable business processes that may include conversational experiences, workflows, or both, using governed tools, integrations, and agentic reasoning. This route asks about audience and channels. The GitHub Copilot Harness is always preferred unless voice or live-human handover is required. Either requirement excludes it and selects the Standard Harness because those capabilities are unavailable in the GitHub Copilot Harness. Fixed-rule steps alone do not select Standard, and website embedding alone does not select SDK. Existing Standard Harness agents can remain on that harness; new/existing lifecycle is a result consideration rather than a question.</p>
      <p>Deeper customization asks which development model is needed. A prompted, employee-facing Microsoft 365 Copilot agent with developer-owned integrations leads to Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit, subject to current tooling and integration validation. A developer-owned runtime leads to Microsoft 365 Agents SDK, with implementation and operational responsibilities explicitly assigned to the team.</p>
      <p>Selecting multiple delivery options establishes a multi-channel requirement. Live-human handover is assessed separately as a service capability.</p>
      <p>Microsoft 365 workspace is offered for employee-only audiences. Selecting customers or a mixed audience disables that option and removes any previous selection of it, while retaining other selected channels.</p>
      <h3>Shortlist and remaining checks</h3>
      <p>Recommendations and alternatives come only from active platforms within the selected approach. Unconfirmed capabilities stay under “Requires confirmation”; incompatible requirements appear under “Excluded.” Platforms outside the approach are counted separately. The guide never silently substitutes a different approach when requirements conflict.</p>
      <p>Unanswered questions impose no capability restrictions. Requirements already explicit in the approach are described as its scope, rather than recorded as answers to skipped questions. Each route uses two or three questions, including the approach selection.</p>
      <p>Non-differentiating checks move to the result’s prerequisites and next steps. They remain unassessed, including applicable scheduling, advanced reasoning, integrations, hosting, and operational controls. A recommendation identifies a starting point and does not certify production readiness.</p>
      <p>Unavailable answer choices stay visible in their original positions, with a brief inline explanation of which earlier answer to change. Only enabled choices can be selected. Whole questions are skipped when irrelevant.</p>
      <p>Editing an approach removes hidden or incompatible answers. Returning to an earlier approach does not restore discarded selections. Reset clears the assessment and returns to the approach selection. If no platform remains, review the requirements or change the approach.</p>
      <h3>Capability validation</h3>
      <p>The draft guidance identifies items requiring confirmation, including non-voice publishing-channel combinations in the GitHub Copilot Harness and integration details for Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit. Voice and live-human handover are treated as unsupported by the GitHub Copilot Harness. Validate the applicable environment requirements, together with roadmap statements, costs, model availability, application lifecycle management (ALM), telemetry, and scheduling capabilities.</p>
      <h3>{relatedGuidance.title}</h3>
      <p>{relatedGuidance.description}</p>
      <h3>Scope and assumptions</h3>
      <p>Within deeper customization, selecting the Microsoft 365 Copilot managed development model excludes Microsoft 365 Agents SDK because the development team must provide and operate its runtime. Selecting a developer-owned runtime excludes Toolkit.</p>
      <p>Within this guidance, Agent Builder, Cowork, and Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit are evaluated for employee scenarios in Microsoft 365. Customer-facing, voice, and custom-client scenarios for these options require separate assessment. For Microsoft 365 Agents SDK, the development team supplies and validates the models, orchestration, and application-specific capabilities.</p>
      <p>Assess website embedding alongside runtime ownership requirements, fixed-rule steps alongside workflow and channel requirements, and prompted tasks alongside the intended job and audience. Each of these factors contributes to platform selection.</p>
      <h3>Data handling</h3>
      <p>Selections remain in browser-tab memory and are cleared when the page is refreshed. The guide operates without user accounts or analytics. The hosting service may retain standard page-access logs.</p>
      <p>Opening a step or recommendation feedback link sends your current selections to GitHub through URL parameters to fill an editable issue form. Recommendation feedback also includes the result and items to validate. The URL may remain in browser history. General feedback includes no selections. A GitHub account is required to submit feedback; submitted issues are public. Review or remove context before submitting, and do not include confidential information.</p>
      <a class="button button-primary" href={withBase()}>Assess a use case</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
